@title Lease or Buy the Press
@subtitle Decision on the new digital press for the commercial print shop
@author Lucinda Marsh, Owner
@date Meeting 2026-11-12

kpi Recommendation: lease | +$17,600 | over four years vs buying | bad

We recommend leasing the new press. It costs **$17,600 more** than buying over four years, but it avoids a $412,000 cash outlay while we need cash for the new bindery. The press prints about 2.4 million pages a month.

# Options
table = Item,Buy,Lease; Upfront cash,"$412,000 purchase","$15,300 deposit (two payments)"; Regular cost,"$14,400 a year service","$7,650 a month, service included"; Term,Own outright,48 months; End of term,"Resell, expected $120,000","Optional buyout for $95,000"

# Four-year cost
data cost = option,usd; Buy,349600; Lease,367200
chart bar cost unit=$ title="Net four-year cost" focus="Lease"

table = Line,Buy,Lease; Purchase or payments,"$412,000","$367,200 (48 × $7,650)"; Service,"$57,600","included"; Resale after four years,"−$120,000",—; **Net four-year cost**,"**$349,600**","**$367,200**"

The lease is $17,600 more over the term, about 0.015 cents per page at 2.4 million pages a month. The optional $95,000 buyout is not included in the lease figure.

# Risks
- **Buy:** ties up cash we need for the new bindery.
- **Buy:** the $120,000 resale value is a guess; a lower resale price raises the real cost of buying.
- **Lease:** locks us in for 48 months.
- **Lease:** the $95,000 buyout price is fixed today, so we know the cost of keeping the press at the end.

# Recommendation
! Lease the press. It costs $17,600 more over four years than buying, and we accept that premium to keep cash free for the bindery and to avoid betting on a resale value nobody can promise.
